[
 'slug' => 'introduction-to-directing', 'title' => 'Introduction to Directing', 'level' => 'Beginner', 'category' => 'Directing', 'duration' => '3 hours', 'featured' => 1,
 'summary' => 'Understand what a director does, from vision and script analysis to shot lists and running a set.',
 'description' => 'The director leads the creative vision of a film. This course introduces the director’s responsibilities, the preparation that makes a shoot run smoothly and the basic language of coverage.',
 'outcomes' => "Describe the director’s role in each stage of filmmaking\nBreak down a script and write a clear director’s vision\nPrepare a shot list and simple storyboard\nPlan scene coverage (master, medium, close-up)\nCommunicate clearly with cast and crew on set",
 'lessons' => [
  ['What a director does', 14, "<p>The director is responsible for the <strong>creative vision</strong> of the film and for guiding the cast and crew to realise it. The producer manages money, people and schedules; the director manages story, performances and how the film looks and sounds.</p><ul><li><strong>Development:</strong> choose or shape the story and find the film’s central idea.</li><li><strong>Pre-production:</strong> cast actors, scout locations, plan shots and rehearse.</li><li><strong>Production:</strong> lead the set and make creative decisions quickly.</li><li><strong>Post-production:</strong> work with the editor, sound and colour teams to shape the final film.</li></ul><p>Good directors listen. Your cinematographer, designer and actors will have ideas that improve yours.</p>"],
  ['Script analysis and director’s vision', 18, "<p>Before planning shots, read the script several times. Ask:</p><ul><li><strong>What is this story really about?</strong> (the theme, in one sentence)</li><li><strong>Who wants what?</strong> Each main character has a want and something in the way.</li><li><strong>How should it feel?</strong> Tense, warm, humorous, dreamlike?</li></ul><p>Write a <strong>director’s statement</strong> of one page: the theme, the tone, the look (colours, camera style), the sound and two or three reference films or photographs. This becomes the guide you share with your crew so everybody pulls in the same direction.</p>"],
  ['Shot lists and storyboards', 18, "<p>A <strong>shot list</strong> lists every shot you need, scene by scene: shot size, camera angle or movement, who is in frame and any notes. A <strong>storyboard</strong> is a drawing (stick figures are fine) of each shot.</p><ul><li>Plan the <strong>must-have shots</strong> first; add extras if time allows.</li><li>Group shots by <strong>camera position and lighting set-up</strong> to save time on the day.</li><li>Think about how shots will <strong>cut together</strong>: avoid two shots of nearly the same size in a row.</li></ul>"],
  ['Coverage and running the set', 20, "<p><strong>Coverage</strong> means filming a scene from several angles so the editor has choices. A common pattern is:</p><ol><li><strong>Master shot:</strong> a wide shot of the whole scene, performed all the way through.</li><li><strong>Medium shots / over-the-shoulder shots</strong> of each actor.</li><li><strong>Close-ups</strong> of key moments, and cutaways (hands, objects, details).</li></ol><h4>On set</h4><ul><li>Start with a short <strong>briefing</strong>: what we are shooting, in what order and why.</li><li>Give actors direction privately and calmly; give crew instructions clearly.</li><li>Check <strong>continuity</strong> (props, costume, positions) between takes.</li><li>Before wrapping a scene, ask: <em>do I have everything I need?</em></li></ul>"],
 ],
 'questions' => [
  ['Who is mainly responsible for a film’s creative vision?', ['The producer', 'The director', 'The caterer', 'The distributor'], 1, 'The director leads the creative vision.'],
  ['What is a director’s statement?', ['A legal contract', 'A short document describing the film’s theme, tone and look', 'A list of budget items', 'A call sheet'], 1, 'It aligns the whole team on the vision.'],
  ['A shot list is…', ['A list of every shot needed for each scene', 'A list of actors’ names', 'A music playlist', 'The film’s final credits'], 0, 'It plans shots scene by scene.'],
  ['What is “coverage”?', ['Insurance for the shoot', 'Filming a scene from several angles', 'Press coverage of the film', 'The camera’s battery life'], 1, 'Coverage gives editors choices.'],
  ['What is a master shot?', ['A close-up of the lead actor', 'A wide shot capturing the whole scene', 'The final shot of the film', 'A shot taken by the producer'], 1, 'It shows the full action of the scene.'],
  ['Why group shots by lighting set-up on the shoot day?', ['To save time', 'To make the film longer', 'To use more equipment', 'To avoid rehearsals'], 0, 'Moving lights is slow; grouping saves time.'],
  ['A storyboard is…', ['A drawing of each planned shot', 'The script', 'A cast list', 'A sound mix'], 0, 'Even simple sketches help planning.'],
  ['During pre-production a director typically…', ['Casts actors, scouts locations and plans shots', 'Edits the final film', 'Delivers the film to cinemas', 'Prints the posters'], 0, 'Pre-production is about planning.'],
  ['Which is the best way to give an actor direction?', ['Shout across the set', 'Speak privately, clearly and calmly', 'Send a text message later', 'Avoid giving any direction'], 1, 'Private, calm direction protects confidence.'],
  ['Why check continuity between takes?', ['So props, costume and positions match when edited', 'To make the shoot slower', 'To avoid using the script', 'For the catering team'], 0, 'Mismatches distract audiences in the cut.'],
 ],
],
[
 'slug' => 'directing-actors-and-scenes', 'title' => 'Directing Actors and Scenes', 'level' => 'Intermediate', 'category' => 'Directing', 'duration' => '3.5 hours',
 'summary' => 'Work with actors, stage a scene, keep the camera on the right side of the action and get performances you can cut.',
 'description' => 'Once you know the basics of directing, the next step is getting strong performances and staging scenes clearly. This course covers objectives and obstacles, giving useful direction, blocking and the 180-degree rule.',
 'outcomes' => "Break a scene into objectives, obstacles and stakes\nGive actors clear, playable direction\nBlock a scene and place the camera using the 180-degree rule\nRun an efficient rehearsal\nShoot scenes so performances will match in the edit",
 'lessons' => [
  ['Objectives, obstacles and stakes', 16, "<p>Strong scenes have conflict. For every character in a scene, find:</p><ul><li><strong>Objective:</strong> what they want from the other person in this scene.</li><li><strong>Obstacle:</strong> what stands in the way.</li><li><strong>Stakes:</strong> what they lose if they fail.</li></ul><p>When actors know their objective, they play <em>actions</em> (to persuade, to hide, to challenge) instead of just saying lines. Ask each actor: <em>What do you want right now?</em></p>"],
  ['Giving direction that helps', 16, "<ul><li><strong>Use action verbs:</strong> “Try to convince her” is playable; “Be sadder” is not.</li><li><strong>Avoid line readings</strong> (telling the actor exactly how to say the line). Give a reason or a goal and let them find the sound.</li><li><strong>Give one note at a time</strong> and keep it short.</li><li><strong>Praise what works</strong> before adjusting what does not.</li><li><strong>Change the situation, not the person:</strong> “You’re late and you haven’t told him” changes behaviour naturally.</li></ul>"],
  ['Blocking and the 180-degree rule', 20, "<p><strong>Blocking</strong> is the planned movement of actors and camera in a scene. Good blocking tells the story: who moves toward whom, who has power, who is isolated.</p><p>Imagine a line between two characters. Keep all camera positions on <strong>one side</strong> of that line (the 180-degree rule). If you cross it, left and right swap and the audience becomes confused about where people are. If you must cross, do it with a clear camera move or a neutral shot.</p><ul><li>Rehearse blocking <strong>without the camera first</strong>, then decide where to place it.</li><li>Mark actors’ positions on the floor with tape.</li></ul>"],
  ['Rehearsal and matching performances', 18, "<ul><li><strong>Rehearse before the day</strong> if you can: read through, talk about objectives and try the scene in different ways.</li><li>On set, rehearse the movement quickly with crew present, then shoot.</li><li>Actors must repeat actions at the <strong>same moments</strong> in each angle (for example, picking up a cup on the same line) so shots cut together. This is called <strong>continuity of performance</strong>.</li><li>Do a few takes, then change something small so you have <strong>variety</strong> for the editor.</li><li>Always shoot a <strong>safety take</strong> if you are unsure.</li></ul>"],
 ],
 'questions' => [
  ['In a scene, a character’s “objective” is…', ['What they want from the other character', 'Their costume', 'The camera angle', 'The location'], 0, 'An objective is what the character wants in the scene.'],
  ['Which direction is most playable for an actor?', ['“Be sadder”', '“Try to make her stay”', '“More emotion”', '“Do it better”'], 1, 'Action verbs and goals give actors something to do.'],
  ['A “line reading” is…', ['Reading the script aloud', 'Telling an actor exactly how to say a line', 'A script supervisor’s report', 'A type of rehearsal'], 1, 'It is usually unhelpful because it takes the performance away from the actor.'],
  ['What are “stakes”?', ['What a character loses if they fail', 'The size of the budget', 'The number of takes', 'The length of the scene'], 0, 'Stakes make the scene matter.'],
  ['The 180-degree rule says that…', ['You should keep the camera on one side of the line between characters', 'Every shot must be 180 seconds long', 'The camera must turn 180 degrees in every shot', 'Actors must face away from the camera'], 0, 'It keeps screen direction consistent.'],
  ['What is blocking?', ['The planned movement of actors and camera', 'Blocking a shot with a flag', 'Cutting a scene', 'Mixing sound'], 0, 'Blocking is staging.'],
  ['Why should actors repeat actions at the same moments in each angle?', ['So shots will match when edited', 'To waste time', 'To keep the lighting the same', 'To save battery'], 0, 'It allows clean cuts between angles.'],
